{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Factor.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Factor.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"factor factor","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Factor.md","fileName":"Factor.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Factor","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Factoring and Algebraic Identities]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Factoring and Algebraic Identities","Polynomial Factorization","Distributive Property","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Factor.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Factor.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"factor factor","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Factor.md","fileName":"Factor.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Factor","heading":"Factor","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A factor is a number or expression multiplied with another to produce a product. In\n\n$$12x^2=3x\\cdot4x,$$\n\n(3x) and (4x) are factors of (12x^2). A factor must divide the product exactly within the number or expression system being used.\n\nRecognizing factors permits cancellation in fractions and supports [[Polynomial Factorization]]. A common factor divides every term of a sum and can be taken outside parentheses through the [[Distributive Property]]. Terms joined by addition are not themselves cancellable factors until the expression is factored.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Factoring and Algebraic Identities","Polynomial Factorization","Distributive Property","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Factor.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Factor.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"factor factor","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Factor.md","fileName":"Factor.md","folder":"6